'''Arawá''' or '''Aruá'''<ref>{{cite web |title=aru {{!}} ISO 639-3 |url=https://iso639-3.sil.org/code/aru |website=iso639-3.sil.org |access-date=4 June 2025 |language=en}}</ref> was a [[extinct language|language]] of [[Brazil]]. Its last speaker died in 1877, before any significant documentation of the language could be completed. The only surviving documentation of the language is an 1869 word list.<ref name="Dixon2006">{{cite journal |last1=Dixon |first1=R. M. W. |title=Annotated Bibliography of the Arawá Language Family to 1950 |journal=International Journal of American Linguistics |date=October 2006 |volume=72 |issue=4 |pages=522–534 |doi=10.1086/513059 |url=https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/513059 |access-date=4 June 2025|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

The language did, however, give its name to the [[language family]] that it was a part of, which is now called the [[Arawan languages]]. This family covers the extant languages [[Kulina language|Kulina]], [[Deni language|Deni]], [[Madí language|Jamamadi]], [[Paumarí language|Paumari]], and [[Zuruahá language|Suruwahá]], all found in Western Brazil and Peru.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Cruz Maciel de Carvalho |first1=Mateus |title=Imperatives in Arawá languages |journal=LIAMES: Línguas Indígenas Americanas |date=20 December 2016 |volume=16 |issue=2 |pages=307–322 |doi=10.20396/liames.v16i2.8646353 |url=https://periodicos.sbu.unicamp.br/ojs/index.php/liames/article/view/8646353 |access-date=4 June 2025 |language=en |issn=2177-7160|doi-access=free }}</ref>

